To setup dev certificate in container execute the below command first
dotnet dev-certs https -ep ./certs/aspnetapp.pfx -p MyPassword123!  OR
dotnet dev-certs https -ep "C:\certs\aspnetapp.pfx" -p MyPassword123!
and then export this certificate to the container using Env variables

Go to github under repositories --> Actions --> Runners --> New Runner --> New Self Hosted Runner 
and follow the instructions that appear there

You have to install the runner in your maching in c:/actions-runner and then configure the runner to connect to the
github workflows. So that whenever you push your code, the workflow gets triggered and the runner which is like 
listener will run the CI/CD script.

The CI/CD will checkoout the repository on push trigger and then restart the docker-compose script and then the control is
transfered to the docker-compose script. 

Docker compose is like contanier orchestration similar to kubernetes and it will configure each container and start the 
container using the docker file. It will create network between containers so that it can talk to each other, configure ports, 
certificates and volumes.

Docker-file is there for each microservice and its job is to copy the code from the local machine to the container, 
restore depedencies, build and publish the app in the container.

C:\actions-runner --> This is the location where you have installed the runner and configured it to connect to github workflows.
C:\certs --> This is the location where you have exported the dev certificate and this will be used in the docker compose file 
			 to copy the certificate to the container and then use it in the application.

./config.cmd remove --> This command is used to remove the runner from the machine and disconnect it from github workflows.
./run.cmd --> This command is used to start the runner and it will start listening for the workflow triggers. Whenever you push your code, 
			 the workflow gets triggered and the runner will run the CI/CD script.

